---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import site from "../data/site.json";
import { u } from "../lib/path.js";

const img = (p, w = 1000) => `${site.cdn}${p}?format=${w}w`;

const description =
  "The Churchman Lab studies two coupled problems: how the life cycle of a nuclear mRNA is controlled from transcription through degradation, and how the nuclear and mitochondrial genomes coordinate expression to build the OXPHOS machinery. We develop the quantitative methods needed to measure both.";

const jsonld = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: "Research — Churchman Lab",
  url: `${site.url}/research`,
  description,
  about: { "@id": `${site.url}/#lab` },
  mainEntity: {
    "@type": "ResearchProject",
    name: "Gene regulation from the nucleus to the mitochondria",
    description,
    parentOrganization: { "@id": `${site.url}/#lab` },
  },
};
---

<Base title="Research" description={description} jsonld={jsonld}>
  <section class="section" style="padding-bottom:34px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
      <h1>Two genomes, one proteome</h1>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          Eukaryotic cells run gene expression out of two separate genomes — the nuclear one and the small circular
          one inside mitochondria — using entirely different machinery, and they have to keep the output balanced.
          We want to know how.
        </p>
        <p>
          The lab works on this from two directions. One is the <a href="#mrna-life-cycle">life cycle of a nuclear
          mRNA</a>: how it is transcribed and processed on chromatin, whether it is exported or destroyed in the
          nucleus, how fast it moves between compartments, and what determines all of that. The other is
          <a href="#mitonuclear">mitonuclear co-regulation</a>: how the thirteen proteins encoded by human mtDNA stay
          in step with the hundreds of nuclear-encoded subunits they have to assemble with.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both questions were, until recently, hard to ask because nobody could measure the relevant quantities.
          So a large fraction of what we do is method development — NET-seq, nano-COP, subcellular TimeLapse-seq,
          mitoribosome profiling, mtFiber-seq. We publish the protocols and the code, because a method that only
          works in one lab is not much of a method.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="box" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:32px">
        <div>
          <h3 style="margin-top:0">If you are a prospective student or postdoc</h3>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
            Each section below ends with the questions we do not yet have answers to. Those are the projects. If one
            of them is the reason you cannot sleep, say so in your email — it is the most useful thing you can tell
            us.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="margin-top:0">If you are a collaborator or journalist</h3>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
            The short version: cells make thousands of RNA molecules a minute and control every step of what happens
            to them. We invent ways to watch those steps in living cells, including inside mitochondria, where
            failures cause metabolic and neurodegenerative disease.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ nuclear mRNA life cycle ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="mrna-life-cycle">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Programme one</p>
        <h2>The nuclear mRNA life cycle</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          mRNA life cycles are dynamic and diverse. Before translation an mRNA is released from chromatin and
          exported from the nucleus; in the cytoplasm ribosomes load onto it and it is eventually degraded.
          Regulatory mechanisms control every one of those transitions — and we can now measure them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <img
        src={img("/f033b8f6-8aeb-429b-a7d5-c975d5cda3de/Figure_3.png", 1400)}
        alt="Schematic of the mRNA life cycle across subcellular compartments"
        loading="lazy"
        style="border-radius:3px;margin-bottom:44px"
      />

      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h3>How are pre-mRNAs transcribed and processed on chromatin?</h3>
          <p class="prose">
            Human genes carry many introns that must be excised to make a functional mRNA. We built direct RNA
            nanopore sequencing methods — nanopore analysis of co-transcriptional processing (nano-COP) — to watch
            pre-mRNA processing without the length biases that come with cDNA synthesis and PCR.
          </p>
          <p class="prose">
            About 60% of introns are removed co-transcriptionally, while the pre-mRNA is still attached to Pol II,
            but splicing catalysis largely happens after Pol II has travelled thousands more nucleotides. Looking
            across whole transcripts, we found introns tend to be excised in one or occasionally two preferred
            orders, constant across cell types and alternative splicing, and identified <em>cis</em>-elements that
            control that order.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <img src={img("/4ad0a382-aea2-4fb5-a51d-9e3970b0d836/timelapse-seq.png", 800)} alt="Subcellular TimeLapse-seq schematic" loading="lazy" style="border-radius:3px" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="box" style="margin-top:34px">
        <h4>Open questions</h4>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0;color:var(--ink-2)">
          How fast are RNA binding proteins and spliceosomal subunits loaded onto nascent RNA? How do
          <em>cis</em>-elements control splicing order?
        </p>
        <p style="margin:.9rem 0 0;font-size:.86rem;color:var(--ink-3)">
          Background reading: Drexler, Choquet &amp; Churchman, <em>Mol Cell</em> 2020 · Choquet et al.,
          <em>Nat Struct Mol Biol</em> 2023
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top:52px">
        <h3>How and why are mRNAs targeted for nuclear degradation?</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Textbooks say mRNAs get exported. But the nuclear RNA exosome degrades introns, antisense RNAs and other
          non-coding RNAs, and we found nuclear mRNA degradation plays a much broader regulatory role than anyone
          expected.
        </p>
        <p class="prose">
          Using subcellular TimeLapse-seq we tracked the age of RNAs as they crossed subcellular compartments and
          derived half-lives at subcellular resolution with a Bayesian model — chromatin release, nuclear export,
          polysome loading, and nuclear and cytoplasmic degradation rates, for every human mRNA. For roughly 10% of
          genes a simple life-cycle model does not fit; you need substantial nuclear degradation. We call these
          PUNDs — predicted to undergo nuclear degradation — and for these genes more than 85% of transcripts are
          never exported at all. Many of them are themselves genes of RNA biology: ribosomal subunits, splicing
          factors, nuclear export factors. PUND transcripts share features — incomplete splicing, long poly(A)
          tails — that may be how the degradation machinery finds them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="box" style="margin-top:20px">
        <h4>Open questions</h4>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0;color:var(--ink-2)">
          How are PUND transcripts recognised and targeted for nuclear decay? What is nuclear mRNA degradation
          actually <em>for</em>?
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top:52px">
        <h3>How is mRNA subcellular localisation controlled?</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          RNAs do not all move through the cell at the same speed — flow rates vary more than a hundredfold between
          genes and compartments. Functionally related genes share similar flow rates, and the targets of many RNA
          binding proteins move differently from everything else. We measured poly(A) tails with subcellular
          resolution and showed tail length reflects subcellular half-life, then used machine learning to find the
          genetic and molecular features that predict flow, including transcription factors and sequence elements.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="box" style="margin-top:20px">
        <h4>Open questions</h4>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0;color:var(--ink-2)">
          How do mRNAs flow to organelles and other compartments? How do transcript isoforms change RNA flow?
        </p>
        <p style="margin:.9rem 0 0;font-size:.86rem;color:var(--ink-3)">
          Background reading: Ietswaart et al., <em>Mol Cell</em> 2024
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ single molecule ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="single-molecule">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
        <h2>Single-molecule views of transcription and chromatin</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Population averages hide coordination. Using long-read chromatin fiber sequencing (Fiber-seq) we can
            place RNA polymerases within their native chromatin context at single-molecule and near
            single-nucleotide resolution along fibers up to 30 kb long.
          </p>
          <p>
            What emerges is pervasive direct coordination — and anti-coordination — between nearby Pol II genes,
            Pol III genes, transcribed enhancers and insulator elements. That coordination is spatially limited and
            depends on whether insulators are simultaneously bound to the same DNA molecule. These are relationships
            you simply cannot see in bulk data.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <img src={img("/d53c123a-2206-44d5-8b6f-3512215aee5b/AGTD+poster%404x.png", 800)} alt="Single-molecule chromatin fiber sequencing" loading="lazy" style="border-radius:3px" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ mitonuclear ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="mitonuclear">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Programme two</p>
        <h2>Mitonuclear co-regulation</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Human mtDNA descends from a eubacterial genome and has shrunk to 16.5 kb encoding just thirteen proteins,
          all of them core subunits of the OXPHOS complexes. Every other subunit is nuclear-encoded. Get the balance
          wrong and subunits accumulate discordantly — mitonuclear imbalance. How does a cell coordinate expression
          across two systems with entirely separate machinery?
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Our first work here asked simply whether the two systems are coordinated at all. By adapting ribosome
            profiling to mitochondrial protein synthesis we showed that translation of nuclear- and
            mitochondrial-encoded OXPHOS subunits is balanced, in both yeast and human cells.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>mtDNA packaging.</strong> Human cells carry hundreds of mtDNA molecules across the mitochondrial
            network. Each is over 5 µm long and compressed into a 100 nm nucleoprotein complex — a nucleoid. Using
            mtFiber-seq we measured accessibility of full-length mtDNA molecules across human cell types and found
            most nucleoids are inaccessible, with the fraction varying by cell type and mitochondrial function.
            Unlike nuclear compaction, mtDNA accessibility is largely all-or-none, which suggests genome activation
            is itself a layer of regulation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The mitochondrial mRNA life cycle.</strong> Two promoters in the non-coding region generate
            near-genome-length polycistronic transcripts, cleaved into individual mRNAs with little or no 5′ UTR. We
            measured production, processing, ribosome arrival and turnover with TimeLapse-seq and direct nanopore
            RNA sequencing, then modelled protein synthesis — and found that turnover rate and translation
            efficiency, not transcription, mostly account for mitochondrial protein synthesis levels.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <img src={img("/57198b25-5b5c-417a-a9cf-18f79aff0bce/mtDNA_scheme.png", 800)} alt="Schematic of mitochondrial DNA organisation" loading="lazy" style="border-radius:3px;margin-bottom:18px" />
          <img src={img("/e1709984-56e8-4bdd-98d9-72022ee3c00c/mitochondria+metabolome2.png", 800)} alt="Mitochondria and the metabolome" loading="lazy" style="border-radius:3px" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top:52px">
        <h3>Which nuclear-encoded factors and metabolites control mitochondrial gene expression?</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          In yeast, translation activators are nuclear-encoded, imported into mitochondria, and promote translation
          of specific mRNAs through their 5′ UTRs. To find the human equivalents we built genome-wide CRISPR screens
          for factors that shift the balance between mitochondrial-encoded COX1 and nuclear-encoded COX4. The screens
          recovered known regulators such as TACO1 — whose mutations cause Leigh syndrome — alongside genes with no
          established mitochondrial role. We are currently pursuing PREPL, highly expressed in brain, and NME6, a
          nucleotide diphosphate kinase.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top:44px">
        <h3>How does mitonuclear co-regulation work in neurons?</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Neurons depend heavily on mitochondrial function and have a complex, polarised morphology that makes
          balanced expression across two genomes genuinely hard. We are asking whether that structure makes neurons
          more vulnerable to mitonuclear imbalance under genetic or physiological stress — and whether that
          vulnerability is relevant to neurodegenerative disease.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="box" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>Open questions</h4>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0;color:var(--ink-2)">
          What is the spatial organisation of mtDNA regulation and expression? How is mitochondrial translation
          regulated? Do mitochondrial metabolite pools influence mitochondrial gene regulation? Is nuclear- and
          mitochondrial-encoded expression balanced in neurons, and does that balance differ between the soma and
          neurite-localised mitochondria?
        </p>
        <p style="margin:.9rem 0 0;font-size:.86rem;color:var(--ink-3)">
          Background reading: Couvillion et al., <em>Nature</em> 2016 · Soto et al., <em>Genome Biol</em> 2022 ·
          Kramer &amp; Prakash et al., <em>Nat Cell Biol</em> 2023 · Isaac et al., <em>Nat Struct Mol Biol</em> 2024 ·
          McShane et al., <em>Mol Cell</em> 2024
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn" href={u("/join")}>Work on these questions with us</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={u("/publications")}>See all publications</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={u("/tools")}>Methods, code and protocols</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
